"""Extra sections appended to each post's article, to bring every post to full guide depth."""
EXTRA = {
"is-hipages-worth-it": """<h2 id="stay">If you stay on hipages: how to win more of the jobs you pay for</h2><ul><li><strong>Call, do not just message.</strong> The first business to have a real conversation usually wins. Call within minutes, and message only if they do not pick up.</li><li><strong>Be picky about categories and areas.</strong> Turn off job types that rarely pay for themselves and postcodes too far to drive profitably.</li><li><strong>Fill out your profile properly.</strong> Photos of real jobs, your licence details and a clear description make homeowners choose you from the list.</li><li><strong>Quote fast.</strong> A clear written price the same day beats a better price three days later.</li><li><strong>Turn every platform job into a Google review.</strong> That way each job you pay for keeps working for you after the platform is gone.</li></ul><h2 id="homeowners">Where Australian homeowners actually look</h2><p>In renovation forums, homeowners describe searching Google first, reading reviews, and looking for photos of past work before they call. Some say they struggle to find good trades at all because so many have no website or listing. That is why a complete Google Business Profile, a stack of recent reviews and a simple website with your real work on it put you in front of customers who never open a lead app. See <a href="/guides/local-seo-for-trades/">how trades get found on Google Maps</a>.</p>""",
"is-checkatrade-worth-it": """<h2 id="stay">Getting the most from Checkatrade if you stay</h2><ul><li><strong>Respond to every enquiry fast</strong>, by phone where possible. Speed matters most on shared leads.</li><li><strong>Collect reviews on Checkatrade and Google.</strong> Your Checkatrade reviews stay on Checkatrade; Google reviews follow you whatever you decide next year.</li><li><strong>Narrow your categories and areas</strong> so you only pay for work you want.</li><li><strong>Track every job by source</strong> in a simple spreadsheet, so renewal is a decision based on numbers, not a feeling.</li><li><strong>Diary the renewal date</strong> two months ahead, so you have time to decide.</li></ul><h2 id="homeowners">What UK homeowners check besides the badge</h2><p>A vetting badge helps, but homeowners also search your business name on Google, look at your reviews and photos, and check you look established. A trade with a strong Google profile and a clear website can win the same customers without paying for a directory listing. See <a href="/guides/local-seo-for-trades/">local SEO for trades</a> and our guide to <a href="/guides/website-cost-uk/">what a trade website costs in the UK</a>.</p>""",
"are-local-services-ads-worth-it": """<h2 id="ranking">How Google decides who shows first</h2><p>Paying more is not the only lever. Google says ranking and lead cost in Local Services Ads depend on things like your distance to the customer, your reviews and rating, how responsive you are, your business hours and how complete your profile is. In practice that means:</p><ul><li><strong>Reviews matter twice:</strong> once in the ad itself and again in where you rank.</li><li><strong>Missed calls hurt.</strong> Answering quickly helps you rank and stops you paying for leads you never speak to.</li><li><strong>Set your hours honestly.</strong> Ads show when you say you are open, so make sure someone answers then.</li></ul><h2 id="verification">Verification and badges</h2><p>To run Local Services Ads, Google checks your business, usually including licence and insurance details for many trades, and in some cases background checks. Once verified you get a badge on your ad, which builds trust before the customer even calls. Have your paperwork ready before you start; verification can take a while.</p><h2 id="when-not">When Local Services Ads are not worth it</h2><ul><li>You cannot answer the phone during the day.</li><li>Your average job is small and your area is very competitive.</li><li>You already have more work than you can handle from Google and referrals.</li><li>Your profile has very few reviews; build those first.</li></ul>""",
"customer-ghosted-after-quote": """<h2 id="quote-that-wins">How to write a quote that wins more often</h2><ul><li><strong>Lead with the outcome,</strong> not the materials list: “New 60 square metre exposed aggregate driveway, ready to drive on in 7 days.”</li><li><strong>Spell out what is included</strong> and what is not, so cheaper quotes that leave things out look worse next to yours.</li><li><strong>Offer options.</strong> A good, better and best version lets people choose a level instead of choosing a competitor.</li><li><strong>Add proof:</strong> two or three photos of similar jobs and a short review.</li><li><strong>Show the timeline and the payment stages,</strong> so there are no surprises.</li><li><strong>Make it easy to say yes:</strong> a clear next step, a deposit link, a date you can start.</li></ul><h2 id="lost-it">When you lose the job anyway</h2><p>Ask, kindly: “No problem at all. Would you mind telling me what made the difference? It helps me improve.” Most people will answer. If price keeps coming up, you may be quoting the wrong customers; if trust keeps coming up, your reviews and online presence need work. Either way you learn more from one honest answer than from ten silent ones.</p>""",
"how-to-deal-with-time-wasters": """<h2 id="script">A first call script you can use</h2><p>“Thanks for calling. So I can give you a proper idea, can I ask a few quick questions? What are you looking to have done? Roughly when are you hoping to start? Jobs like this usually come in between X and Y; is that around what you were expecting? Great. Could you text me a few photos so I can check a couple of things before I come out? And will anyone else be part of the decision, so they can be there when I visit?”</p><p>Two minutes, friendly, and you know whether the visit is worth it.</p><h2 id="signs">Signs a lead is not serious</h2><ul><li>Will not give any idea of budget, even after hearing a range.</li><li>No timeframe, or “just getting prices for now”.</li><li>Will not send photos or answer simple questions.</li><li>Asks for a detailed breakdown, drawings or a materials list up front.</li><li>Has already had several quotes and only mentions price.</li></ul><p>None of these mean you should be rude. They mean a ballpark by phone instead of a site visit, or a paid visit for complex work. See <a href="/guides/should-you-charge-for-quotes/">should you charge for quotes</a>.</p><h2 id="track">Track it for a month</h2><p>Write down every quote, where the enquiry came from and whether you won it. After a month you will see which sources bring serious customers and which bring time wasters. That tells you where to spend your time and money.</p>""",
"should-you-charge-for-quotes": """<h2 id="how-much">How to set your quote fee</h2><p>Work out what a typical visit really costs you: travel time, time on site, and the time to price and write it up. Multiply by your hourly rate. You do not have to charge all of it; many trades charge enough to cover travel and an hour, then credit it against the job. A fee that covers your costs filters time wasters without scaring off real customers.</p><h2 id="estimate-vs-quote">Estimate, quote or design fee?</h2><ul><li><strong>Estimate or ballpark:</strong> a rough range, usually free, often by phone or from photos.</li><li><strong>Quote:</strong> a firm price for a defined job, sometimes after a paid visit for complex work.</li><li><strong>Design or consultation fee:</strong> for drawings, plans, colour schemes or detailed specifications that have value on their own.</li></ul><p>Being clear which one you are giving helps customers understand why one is free and another is not.</p><h2 id="remote">Remote quoting saves the most time</h2><p>Photos, short videos and video calls let you price many jobs without leaving the yard. A quote form on your website that asks for photos up front does a lot of this work for you, and serious customers like the speed. See <a href="/guides/how-to-deal-with-time-wasters/">how to deal with time wasters</a>.</p>""",
"how-to-get-more-customers": """<h2 id="30-days">A simple 30 day plan</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Do this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Finish your Google Business Profile: category, services, areas, hours, 20 real photos.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Text your last 20 customers for a Google review and a referral.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Contact five builders, designers or property managers with photos of your work.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Make sure your website has a page for each main service and a tap to call button. Ask every new caller how they found you.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>After 30 days you will have more reviews, a stronger listing and a clear picture of where your work comes from.</p><h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes that cost customers</h2><ul><li><strong>Missing calls.</strong> Many people call the next business on the list instead of leaving a message. Call back within minutes or use a call answering service.</li><li><strong>A website that only works on a computer.</strong> Most people search on their phone.</li><li><strong>No prices at all.</strong> Even a rough “jobs like this usually start from” line helps people decide to call.</li><li><strong>Stock photos.</strong> Customers want to see your real work and your real crew.</li><li><strong>Spreading too thin.</strong> Better to be strong on Google in your area than present everywhere and remembered nowhere.</li></ul>""",
"how-to-get-google-reviews": """<h2 id="past-customers">Asking past customers</h2><p>If you have done work before setting up your profile, those customers can still review you. Send a short, personal message: “Hi Tom, it is Dan, I did your fence last spring. I have just set up my Google page and a review from you would really help. Here is the link: [link]. Thanks either way!” Older customers often leave the most detailed reviews because they have lived with the work for a while.</p><h2 id="bad-review">How to reply to a bad review</h2><p>Stay calm, be brief, and write for the next customer who reads it:</p><p>“Thanks for the feedback, Chris. I am sorry the cleanup did not meet your expectations; that is not the standard we aim for. I have called to arrange a return visit this week to sort it out.”</p><p>Never argue publicly or share private details. A calm, helpful reply to a bad review often builds more trust than another five star review.</p><h2 id="habit">Make it a habit, not a campaign</h2><p>The businesses with the most reviews are not the ones who ran a big push once. They ask at the end of every single job, every week. Put it on your job completion checklist next to invoicing, and within a few months the numbers take care of themselves.</p>""",
"slow-season": """<h2 id="by-trade">Slow season ideas by trade</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Trade</th><th>Ideas for quiet months</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HVAC</td><td>Servicing plans, pre season checks, upgrade offers before peak</td></tr><tr><td>Roofing</td><td>Gutter cleaning, roof inspections, storm damage repairs</td></tr><tr><td>Landscaping</td><td>Winter tidy ups, design work for spring, hardscaping that can be done in cooler weather</td></tr><tr><td>Concrete</td><td>Commercial and builder work, booking spring driveways early</td></tr><tr><td>Painting</td><td>Interior work, commercial repaints while offices are quiet</td></tr><tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Safety checks, lighting upgrades, EV charger installs</td></tr><tr><td>Plumbing</td><td>Hot water services, heating repairs, bathroom renovations</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="pages">Why seasonal pages bring in work</h2><p>When the weather turns, searches like “heater repair near me” or “storm damage roof repair” jump. If your website has a dedicated page for that service, mentioning the areas you cover, Google has something to show. Trades without those pages miss the spike entirely, no matter how good their work is.</p>""",
"how-to-get-paid-on-time": """<h2 id="wording">Example payment terms</h2><p>Adapt something like this for your quotes (and check it suits the rules where you work):</p><p><em>“A deposit of [amount] is due to confirm your booking and before materials are ordered. Progress payments are due on completion of each stage listed above. The final invoice is due within 7 days of completion. Changes to the scope will be quoted in writing and agreed before the work is done. This quote is valid for 14 days.”</em></p><h2 id="why-late">Why customers pay late</h2><ul><li><strong>The invoice arrived weeks later</strong> and the job no longer feels urgent.</li><li><strong>Paying is awkward:</strong> bank details buried in a PDF, no payment link.</li><li><strong>There is a dispute</strong> about a variation that was never agreed in writing.</li><li><strong>Cash flow problems</strong> on their side, especially with builders and commercial clients.</li></ul><p>Most of these are fixed by invoicing the same day, offering easy payment and agreeing changes in writing.</p><h2 id="commercial">Builders and commercial clients</h2><p>Commercial work often comes with longer payment terms. Agree them before you start, invoice exactly as the contract asks, and keep copies of every approval. If a builder is often late, factor that into your price or limit how much work you take from them at once.</p>""",
}
